WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.480
A Schwann cell meets a large axon and wraps it in dozens of turns.

2
00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:10.540
Beside a thin axon, it wraps nothing.

3
00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:12.500
Who decides?

4
00:00:13.183 --> 00:00:17.683
In the 1970s, two laboratories swapped Schwann cells between nerves.

5
00:00:18.683 --> 00:00:23.623
Schwann cells from a nerve with bare axons now meet axons that are normally

6
00:00:23.623 --> 00:00:25.863
myelinated. What do they do?

7
00:00:32.233 --> 00:00:34.253
They myelinated their new axons.

8
00:00:35.113 --> 00:00:37.213
The reverse swap gave the reverse result.

9
00:00:38.093 --> 00:00:40.113
In peripheral nerve, the axon decides.

10
00:00:44.617 --> 00:00:46.557
So the axon must carry a signal.

11
00:00:47.437 --> 00:00:51.777
The main one is neuregulin-1 type III, a protein on the axon's surface.

12
00:00:52.817 --> 00:00:57.277
It stays tethered near the axon membrane, so a Schwann cell reads it mainly by

13
00:00:57.277 --> 00:00:58.277
touch.

14
00:00:58.567 --> 00:01:03.107
Two receptors on the Schwann cell read it, ErbB3 and ErbB2.

15
00:01:03.867 --> 00:01:04.867
Paired, they signal.

16
00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:08.640
A large axon has more membrane per unit length.

17
00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:12.260
If the density is similar, it carries more type III.

18
00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:14.260
Could the amount be the message?

19
00:01:15.333 --> 00:01:17.373
Give mice half the normal type III.

20
00:01:18.193 --> 00:01:20.333
What happens to the sheath, and to the g ratio?

21
00:01:26.883 --> 00:01:30.963
The sheaths were thinner for the axon's size, so the g ratio went up.

22
00:01:31.883 --> 00:01:34.123
Now let neurons make extra type III.

23
00:01:35.083 --> 00:01:36.083
Predict the sheath.

24
00:01:42.133 --> 00:01:44.513
Thicker myelin, and a lower g ratio.

25
00:01:45.373 --> 00:01:49.033
The dose changed in both directions, and the sheath followed both times.

26
00:01:53.433 --> 00:01:56.033
Sympathetic axons normally stay bare.

27
00:01:56.893 --> 00:02:00.333
Make their neurons produce extra type III, and add Schwann cells.

28
00:02:01.193 --> 00:02:02.193
What then?

29
00:02:08.117 --> 00:02:10.037
The Schwann cells myelinated them.

30
00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:15.520
Giving extra signal to axons that would stay bare changed what the glia did.

31
00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:17.800
The outcome followed the delivery.

32
00:02:18.883 --> 00:02:21.003
That makes type III instructive.

33
00:02:21.483 --> 00:02:26.103
It is the same permissive-versus-instructive test as in the neurotrophic hypothesis

34
00:02:26.103 --> 00:02:27.103
lesson.

35
00:02:27.567 --> 00:02:30.987
Below a threshold of type III, an axon stays bare.

36
00:02:31.887 --> 00:02:34.047
Above it, more type III helps thicken the sheath.

37
00:02:34.917 --> 00:02:39.557
If big axons carry more of it, one readout could match the wrap to the axon.

38
00:02:40.277 --> 00:02:42.157
That is a proposal, not yet shown.

39
00:02:43.167 --> 00:02:45.827
The signal is decisive, but not enough alone.

40
00:02:46.707 --> 00:02:52.147
The Schwann cell also needs its basal lamina, and its own receptor, Gpr126.

41
00:02:53.507 --> 00:02:55.567
Both are required, and not mere on-switches.

42
00:02:56.647 --> 00:02:59.967
The basal lamina also tunes the response to neuregulin.

43
00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:05.740
In nerve, the axon sets the dose, and the dose is the main signal for the wrap.
